%I A399996 #4 Oct 25 2026 21:00:16
%S A399996 1,4,18,97,432,1808,7651,30877,124835,498744,1996242,7967877,31846658,
%T A399996 127281364
%N A399996 Number of free polyominoes with 2n cells that can be dissected into two congruent polyominoes.
%C A399996 Each half is necessarily an n-omino. Here congruent means equal up to rotation, reflection and translation, so the two halves may be mirror images of each other. We call such a polyomino "halvable".
%C A399996 Every polyomino that has a mirror axis lying along grid lines is halvable: each side of the axis is connected, because any connected component of one side has to touch the axis (otherwise it would be a component of the whole polyomino), and across the axis cells are adjacent only to their own mirror images. The two sides of such a polyomino form a halving into mirror-image halves. Consequently A000105(n)/2 <= a(n) <= 8 * A000105(n) * (2n+1)^2.
%C A399996 A halvable polyomino may admit several halvings. The 2 X 3 rectangle is the smallest polyomino with two essentially different halvings: into two I-trominoes, or into two L-trominoes. The number of (2n)-ominoes with at least two distinct halvings are 0, 1, 2, 11, 29, 118 for n = 1..6.
%C A399996 The T-tetromino is the smallest polyomino with an even number of cells that is not halvable. The number of (2n)-ominoes that are not halvable is A000105(2n) - a(n).
%H A399996 Robert Dougherty-Bliss, Natalya Ter-Saakov, and Doron Zeilberger, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.16153">Cutting 4 by n grids into two congruent pieces</a>, arXiv:2510.16153 [math.CO], 2025-2026.
